Description: Resizable BAR for AMD GPUs behind PCIe switches
 Enlarges the CPU-visible VRAM aperture (BAR0) of every AMD GPU handled by
 the amdgpu driver to the largest size the device supports, on machines
 where neither the firmware nor the driver's own resize path can do it:
 GPUs that sit behind a PCIe switch (an on-board PLX switch on a dual-die
 card, an expansion chassis, a Thunderbolt enclosure), several bridge
 windows deep, so that a bare Resizable BAR register write leaves the BAR
 unassigned because the windows above it never grow.
 .
 It programs the new size, removes the highest PCI subtree that contains
 nothing but GPU functions, and rescans that subtree on its own root bus
 with pci=realloc so the kernel re-sizes the bridge windows; nothing else
 on the bus is touched and no global rescan is ever issued. amdgpu is then
 loaded exactly once, behind a bind guard that never hands the driver a
 device whose BAR did not come back (that would hang the boot in an SR-IOV
 mailbox wait). GPUs, Resizable BAR capabilities and bridge topology are
 discovered at run time; GPUs without the capability, and any non-AMD
 device, are left alone.
 .
 Ships a boot-time systemd unit, a modprobe blacklist that defers the
 amdgpu autoload until the unit has run, and a GRUB drop-in adding
 pci=realloc to the kernel command line. Reference platform: dual-die MPX
 modules in an Apple Mac Pro (2019).

